    "submission": "submission.json",
    "summary": "Submitted a combined map handoff and a new candidate on the consequence the first finding excluded.\n\nContext from the index: both findings remain confirmed_in_scope, the heartbeat candidate is now explained, map v4 is the accepted basis, pending_work is empty, and capacity still holds 2 audit units, 4 semantic reviews and 6 experiments.\n\nProduct contents (draft-relative submission.json with the updated map.json):\n\nMap handoff (clarification, with preserves and no challenges): b-follower-append replaces its answered off-thread premise with the sourced conditional relationship — NetworkTransport fast-paths only requests with a nonzero term, no previous entry, no entries and a zero leader commit index, so the off-thread appendEntries takes no log, configuration or commit branch, and the fields it does touch are individually serialized (atomic state/currentTerm, leaderLock, lastContactLock, observers read lock). It keeps two residual gaps: the non-atomic (setState, setCurrentTerm) pair under a concurrent campaign, and the shutdown branch of processHeartbeat returning without answering the RPC. Its sources now also cite net_transport.go 640-745.\n\nNew candidate (continue, disposition concrete_suspicion, preferred_check direct_test): whether a node that adopted a configuration whose entry was never stored can later commit that configuration after regaining leadership with the vote of a fresh peer. The question cites the A2 candidate-election path as the direct behavior with b-config-change and b-commit-quorum as sourced support, keeps the principal fact f-config-membership, and records the two counterevidence points (adoption alone cannot command a majority; processConfigurationLogEntry is the only intended writer) plus the two named discriminators (whether a fresh peer grants the vote; whether the commitment gate can then be satisfied so the phantom configuration becomes committed).\n\nPreflight `validate` returned valid=true with no diagnostics. Acceptance remains with the controller; if another turn is available, the natural next step is to escalate this candidate to an obligation plus a clean-copy check of the consequence path."
